\details{
\subsection{DBSCAN}{

DBSCAN (density-based spatial clustering of applications with noise) does
not need the number of clusters, but two parameters: the radius \code{eps} and
the minimum number of points \code{min_pts}.
\enumerate{
\item A respondent is a \emph{core point} if at least \code{min_pts} respondents
(including itself) lie within the distance \code{eps}.
\item Core points within \code{eps} of each other are connected and form a
cluster; respondents within \code{eps} of a core point (\emph{border points}) are
added to its cluster.
\item All other respondents are \emph{noise} (cluster 0).
}

DBSCAN finds clusters of arbitrary shape and identifies outliers, but uses
a single density threshold for all clusters. \code{min_pts} defaults to twice
the number of clustering variables (a common rule of thumb). \code{eps} defaults
to the "knee" of the sorted k-nearest-neighbor distances, see
\code{\link[=seg_dbscan_eps]{seg_dbscan_eps()}}.
}

\subsection{HDBSCAN}{

HDBSCAN (hierarchical DBSCAN) runs DBSCAN for all values of \code{eps} at once,
builds a hierarchy of the resulting clusters and keeps the most stable
clusters. It only needs \code{min_pts} (the minimum cluster size) and can find
clusters of varying density. It also returns the probability of each
respondent to belong to its cluster (\code{assignment$membership_prob}) and an
outlier score (\code{assignment$outlier_score}, higher = more outlying).
}

\subsection{When to use}{

For data with dense, well separated groups of arbitrary shape, and to
detect outliers. Survey data with many correlated rating scales rarely
contains such dense regions: the methods then find a single cluster (with
some noise) or only noise. They are therefore mainly useful to identify
outliers (e.g. to exclude them before a k-means or PAM segmentation)
rather than for the segmentation itself. With many clustering variables,
distances become similar for all respondents ("curse of dimensionality"),
which makes density-based clustering difficult; consider clustering on a
few principal components instead. The distance matrix of \code{\link[=seg_prep_data]{seg_prep_data()}}
is used if available.
}
}
